Updated:

To address the original question, you can construct the frequency bands "manually" with a while-loop with condition that the remaining (sorted) word list is not empty, with an inner while-loop for the current bin where you keep adding items until both of the things are true: you have collected at least binMax items in the current bin, and the current word is not tied with the previous word. Inside the inner while-loop, remove words from the remaining word list as they are added to the bin. After breaking out of the inner while-loop (inside the main while-loop), you increment the bin number.

Very much pseudocode, but should be standard.

I can't think of an easier way like using some fancy indexing trick in R to break ties for you and construct the bands.

But I think Stefan's point is the heart of it. It reminds me of a talk he gave way back in 2008 (maybe?) on automatically detecting an 'optimal' or 'best' way to group years for historical corpus data. It's much like the frequency bands here. Why 1000? Might there be a data-driven way to automatically detect bins, grouping together words of 'similar' frequencies? One thought that comes to mind is looking at the frequency distribution for any kinks. It's very non-automatic, but it may give you a feel for roughly what you're dealing with, although of course with the Zipfian you already know the distribution. So I don't have a good answer for either # bins or deciding which bin a word should go into. I guess these questions might be aided by considering overall research question.

